   236	and generalised.** Sole affine singularity an ordinary node: `s=1`,
   237	`nu=1`, so (M) reads `sigma * 1 - s_p = d - 1`, i.e.
   238	`sigma - s_p = d-1`. But `sigma <= d-2` and `s_p >= 0`. **Contradiction
   239	for every `d >= 2`.** The promoted theorem
   240	(`c61f0ceb...`, `CONFIRM_W_CORR`) needed `d>=4`, a one-place hypothesis,
   241	a transposition class, corrected Neumann–Rudolph goodness, node
   242	smoothing, Neumann's minimal-Seifert theorem, genus-one graph-knot
   243	classification and the trefoil's meridian rank. (M) needs none of them —
   244	only (H2), (H3) and `d>=2`. This is the strongest available control on
   245	the mechanism: it reproduces a promoted result from a disjoint
   246	direction, and strengthens it.
   247	
   248	**(b) A general node-count obstruction.** Feeding `s_p <= sigma` and
   249	`sigma <= d-2` into (M):
   250	> **`(d-2)*(s-1) >= 2*nu - 1`.**
   251	At `d=4`: **`s >= nu + 1`** — the number of singular points of `A_F`
   252	must strictly exceed its branch excess. In particular at degree four a
   253	single non-unibranch point is impossible unless another singularity
   254	accompanies it, and `s=1` forces `nu=0` (the surviving configuration is
   255	exactly *unicuspidal*, which is the campaign's live one-cusp horn).
   256	
   257	**(c) An exact cusp-local-group law (the attainment instrument).** When
   258	`nu = 0` (all singularities unibranch — the surviving frontier), (M)
   259	collapses to
   260	> **`sum_{p} s_p = sigma*(s-1) + 1`.**
   261	For the charged quartic *transposition* class, `sigma = 2`, so
   262	`sum_p s_p = 2s-1`: **exactly one cusp has `s_p = 1` and every other
   263	cusp has `s_p = 2`.** `s_p = 2 = sigma` forces `H_p = <g>` cyclic of
   264	order two, i.e. *the local knot group of that cusp maps abelianly*: all
   265	its meridians go to the same transposition. For `sigma = 1` (generic
   266	inertia a 3-cycle) every cusp must satisfy `s_p = 1`, forcing every
   267	`H_p` to be cyclic of order three. Either way the cusp local
   268	representations are pinned, and the pinning is checkable directly from
   269	the Puiseux/iterated-torus data the census already carries.
   270	
   271	**(d) The Orevkov budget, re-derived.** Over a generic point of `D`
   272	exactly `sigma` sheets survive and `d - sigma` escape, distributed as
   273	the non-trivial cycles of `g` — cycle length = ramification index of the
   274	corresponding `B_Y` component = dicritical multiplicity. So the total
   275	sheet loss is `d - sigma >= 2` and the promoted "generic branch
   276	partition `(2,1,1)` or `(3,1)`" is exactly "`g != 1`". The `N-1 = 3`
   277	budget at `d=4` is the Euler cost of non-properness. **This discharges
   278	the un-byte-hashed-Orevkov custody risk (F2) for the budget itself.**
   279	
   280	**(e) Structure of the intermediate surface, free.** From Step 0: the
   281	units/localisation sequence
   282	`C[U]^* / C[Y]^* -> Z^{m_Y} -> Cl(Y) -> Cl(A^2)=0` with
   283	`C[Y]^* = C[U]^* = C^*` gives **`Cl(Y) =~ Z^{m_Y}` — free**, where
   284	`m_Y = #`components of `B_Y`. Since `A^2 ⊂ Y` is Zariski-open and
   285	`kappa-bar` does not decrease on opens, **`kappa-bar(Y) = -infty`**;
   286	`pi_1(A^2) ↠ pi_1(Y)` gives **`pi_1(Y)=1`**. For smooth `Y`,
   287	Miyanishi–Sugie/Fujita then give an `A^1`-fibration over `A^1` whose
   288	degenerate fibres satisfy `sum_i (k_i - 1) = m_Y` with **no multiple
   289	single-component fibre** (that would put torsion in `Cl(Y)`), and
   290	`e(Y) = 1 + m_Y`. Two side-effects: `Cl(Y) != 0` re-proves
   291	"`Y` is not `A^2`" in one line, and every torsion-in-`Cl(Y)` /
   292	cyclic-torsor argument on `Y` is dead by theorem.
   293	
   294	### Honest gaps and scope
   295	
   296	- (M) needs (H2)+(H3). At rank four both are promoted; elsewhere they
   297	  are hypotheses. Without (H3), `e(D) = e(D-tilde) - nu` with
   298	  `e(D-tilde) <= 1`, so (M) becomes an inequality in the same direction
   299	  and (a) survives whenever `e(D-tilde) = 1`.
   300	- The `m >= 2` version needs `e(A_F) = sum_i e(D_i) - (identifications)`
   301	  and one `sigma_i` per component; the identity generalises but the
   302	  clean corollaries (a)–(c) do not transfer verbatim. Typed **OPEN**.
   303	- I claimed at one point in derivation that every component of
   304	  `F^{-1}(D)` is finite over `D`; that is **false in general** (a
   305	  component's closure in `Y` may meet `B_Y`), and I do not use it. (M)
   306	  as stated is independent of it. Recording the discarded step so no
   307	  successor re-imports it.
   308	- Smoothness of `Y` is used only in (e), not in (M); for normal `Y`
   309	  run (e) on a resolution containing `U`.
